-- title:
--   The Hecke algebra of S₂(Γ₀(N)) is a finitely generated ℤ-module
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number that is nonzero, and let $S$ be an arbitrary set of natural numbers. Inside the $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphism ring $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}\, S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ of the space of weight-$2$ cusp forms for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$, consider the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), defined as the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra generated by the following set of operators: the endomorphisms `heckeTLin` attached to primes $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$, together with the endomorphisms `heckeULin` attached to primes $q$ with $q \mid N$ and $q \notin S$. The theorem asserts that the underlying $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of this subalgebra, obtained via `Subalgebra.toSubmodule`, is finitely generated; that is, the Hecke ring $\mathbb{T}^{S} = \mathbb{Z}[T_\ell, U_q]$ (indices restricted as above) is generated as a $\mathbb{Z}$-module by finitely many of its elements. No hypothesis is placed on $S$, so the statement covers simultaneously the full Hecke ring ($S = \varnothing$) and the anemic variants obtained by discarding the operators at a set of primes.
--
--   This is the classical integrality and finiteness statement for the weight-$2$ Hecke ring acting on $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$, underlying the existence of a stable integral lattice of cusp forms. It is used downstream in the construction of eigenform ideals and in the arguments producing newforms from residually modular data, notably by [`CuspForm.exists_ne_zero_nsmul_heckeTLin_mem_heckeAlgebra`](thm.html#CuspForm.exists_ne_zero_nsmul_heckeTLin_mem_heckeAlgebra) and [`ModularCurve.exists_isEigenformIdeal_of_isResiduallyModularOfLevel`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_isEigenformIdeal_of_isResiduallyModularOfLevel).
